Andrew Hedges (awhs@uhura.cc.rochester.edu) wrote:

: It's true, though, that students could push this through if they wanted. The
: danger is in simply listening to the majority at the expense of a group of
: students who could really be hurt by this. Where do we draw the line between
: majority rights and minority protection? <--not a rhetorical question.


If a member of a minority group has been oppressed by an action initiated 
by a majority position, then the member of the minority has relief in the 
Constitution. Even if the minority is only one person.

The question arises as to what constitutes oppression (or racism or 
sexism or any other ism one may choose.)

The legal structure of our country is predicated upon intent. Did I 
intend to harm you or was it an oversight that resulted in a harm - 
unintended and unforeseen? We are quickly drifting away from this 
standard of legal adjudication toward a position that action constitutes 
prima facie intent. Individuals are being forced to restrict their 
activities and dialogue because others can't see the reality of situations.

This, I believe, stems from an extension of the 60's militancy mindset 
that has gone awry. Taking a militant position against something is 
respectable. It shows that the holder of the ideology has the courage to 
stand up for principles. Yet, the ideology must be predicated by rational 
thought. Fro instance, at our university (and others as well) there is a 
small faction of blacks who believe that all whites are evil. Therefore, 
anything a white individual does is evil. Similarly, this also applies to 
other radical, fringe philosophies - ACT-UP, PETA, certain splinter 
groups from NOW, KKK, Aryan Nation, etc. All these groups push their one 
narrow agenda at the expense of all logic and rationality.

Oppression has been further re-defined to mean a statement or action that 
is at variance with the ideology of the one complaining. If I hold an 
opinion that Asian investment in US technology is detrimental to the 
survival of our industrial base, then I am now automatically oppressing 
people of Asian descent. Now longer can I present a legitimate economic 
argument in defense of my position. No longer is it necessary for the 
Asian contingent (in this case) to refute my statement with 
counter-facts. All that is needed now is a phone call to the 
anti-discrimination compliance officer on our campus and I am up on 
judicial charges of harassment.

But, to the non-rhetorical question. If I commit an act or issue a 
statement that is potentially oppressive to an individual who holds a 
minority position, that individual should have to clearly show that actual 
harm was committed. Not mental harm either. If you can't take the heat of 
an academic debate, you shouldn't be in academia. This, of course, does 
not protect such speech defined as "fighting words." If I use the term 
nigger, I can reasonable expect that precedent dictates an affray will 
soon erupt. But, what if I use this term among people of like mentality 
and such use gets back to someone who is black> Have I harmed them? Have 
I uttered the term with the knowledge that an affray is likely? No. Yet 
under current policies, I am oppressing the individual.

With the particular MIT situation, if this illustration caused harm - so 
be it. The offended party or parties have every opportunity to present 
evidence to support their claim and seek relief. Yet, the action of 
forcibly removing the cover (as I understand the situation) was not even 
initiated by complaint. It was a pre-emptive decision rendered 
arbitrarily as the result of one individuals perception of potential 
oppressive harm. If this is the case, fine. Prove it. If not, keep the 
cover on the book.

Steve Crisp

