It is also not a question that will have the same answer for everyone.

: The only sort of "hurt" we are talking about is emotional. A person has no
: right -- zero -- to suppress what another person says because he might get
: "hurt" in this sense. That is the only place that the line can be drawn.

As I was growing up this was certainly true. Parents and elementary
school teachers taught children that "sticks and stones may break
my bones, but names will never hurt me". Those who took offense at
names were thought to be sissys.

But this is no longer a cultural icon. Many now think names can hurt.
Just because one group, even the majority group, thinks it is true
will not make it true for all groups.

To complicate matters there are many basic inconsistencies in the
stands taken by some groups. Many times these inconsistencies are
pointed out by someone who is not "picking" on minorities, but on
those who are inconsistent. At various time I have called the same
people (oopppps, folks) Negroes, Afro-Americans, Blacks, and 
African-Americans. In s.c.a.a. I have seen threads on why it is
acceptable for African-Americans to use the word "nigger", but
not for other folks to do it.

I tend to think of myself as liberal on the free speech matter
and would error on the side or more rather than less. On the
other hand I can see a clear need to maintain civil tranquillity.
I just feel lucky to be able to enjoy the irony and humor of
the issue without having to make the ultimate decisions.
