>If a member of a minority group has been oppressed by an action initiated 
>by a majority position, then the member of the minority has relief in the 
>Constitution. Even if the minority is only one person.

      Er, no.  The Constitution prohibits slavery, indentured 
servitude, and a wide range of discriminatory actions by government.  
A narrower range of discriminatory actions by private parties
has been prohibited by various acts of Congress, the 1964 Civil Rights
Act being the most notable.  However, anti-discrimination legislation
can't override the First Amendment.

      This generates periodic clashes, but arguments are over things
like cross-burning and the Klan's march through Skokie, IL, not over
publications. 

